  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The remnants of Isaias will continue to move away \nthrough this evening.\n\nThe remnant low of what was Hurricane Isaias is well offshore\nnow and will continue to move away from our region. High\npressure aloft and at the surface will build in through the\nmiddle of the week. Increasing amounts of sunshine today and\nsome gusty winds will be around for the afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread minor tidal flooding expected Monday\nmorning for much of the Atlantic Ocean coasts in New Jersey and\nDelaware.\n\nWhile we will be moving away from the New Moon, and winds will \nbegin to turn with the passage of the remnant low, tidal levels\nwill remain elevated Monday morning as winds will remain shore\nparallel in many locations. This will trap water along the\ncoast, and particularly in the back bays, resulting in a round \nof widespread minor tidal flooding across most of the Atlantic \nOcean coast in New Jersey and Delaware as well as along the \nDelaware Bay shore in Kent County, Delaware. \n\nSpotty minor tidal flooding is also possible Monday morning \nalong the remainder of the Delaware Bay coasts, the tidal \nDelaware River, the Raritan Bay coast, and the Atlantic Ocean \ncoast in Monmouth County. \n\nNo tidal flooding concerns expected along the Chesapeake Bay.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Increasing temperatures and humid conditions through \nmidweek with some shower chances. The pattern becomes more active \ntowards the end of the week and into the start of the weekend. By \nthe weekend, temperatures get much cooler.\n\nAn expansive mid and upper-level ridge well to our south will keep \nthe flow more zonal through much of this coming week. There will be \nmultiple pieces of upper-level energy moving through the region \nalong with a cold front leading to some shower chances this week. A \ncold front sinks south into the area Tuesday night and into \nWednesday as a mid-level shortwave also moves through the region. \nThis may lead to some isolated showers Tuesday evening into Tuesday \nnight. However, any precipitation currently looks limited. The cold \nfront may not fully clear our area until later in the day Wednesday. \nAdditional isolated showers are possible Wednesday evening into \nWednesday night as more upper-level energy moves through the region. \nBy Thursday, it is drier as high pressure builds in. In terms of \ntemperatures, on Tuesday highs reach the 70s for many with humid \nconditions. On Wednesday, highs mainly reach the mid 70s to mid 80s \nwith humid conditions continuing. Behind the cold front, highs on \nThursday are in the 60s for most with lower humidity values.\n\nBy Thursday night, the upper-level pattern begins to become more \namplified. An upper-level trough approaches the region from the west \nfor the end of the week and moves into the Northeast this weekend. \nThere is a large amount of variation among model guidance given this \nbeing towards the end of the long term period. As of right now, at \nthe surface, a deepening low pressure system in Canada will continue \nto move eastward as it strengthens. This low pressure system \ncurrently looks to bring rain to the area during the time period of \nThursday night through Friday night. A strong cold front looks to \nmove through sometime Friday leading to much cooler conditions this \nweekend. Right now, highs on Friday are mainly in the upper 60s\nto mid 70s. Over the weekend, forecast highs are mainly in the \n50s. It will also be a windy period due to the deepening low to \nthe north and an expansive high building in from the west \nleading to a tight pressure gradient across the region.",
